 "output": {
  "decoded_text_skip_special_tokens": "The weather service issued its seasonal outlook on the 9th of March, and the numbers were unusual. In the northern valleys, the average March temperature came in at 3.4 degrees Celsius, roughly 2.1 degrees below the 30-year mean, and snow cover reached 41 centimeters at the 1,200-meter station, the deepest reading since 2014. Rainfall across the eastern plain totaled 118 millimeters for the month, about 76 percent of the normal figure, and the river gauge at Millbrook recorded a peak flow of 62 cubic meters per second on the 20th. Falling to 24 by the end of the month, farmers responded quickly. Sowing on the heavy clay soil slipped by an average of 120 days, from the 18th of April to the 30th, and seed rates rose from 145 kilograms per hectare to 168, a 16 percent increase, because germination was expected to be uneven. Two growers in the Kesteven district reported that their pumps ran for 340 hours in March alone, nearly double the 180 hours logged a year earlier, and the cooperative ordered nine additional sprinkler units at a cost of 2,350 pounds each. And nobody notices a cistern.",
